<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shining Path</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1969">1969</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sendero Luminoso, Communist Party of Peru – Shining Path</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shining Path is a Maoist insurgent organisation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, founded around 1969 by the philosophy professor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abimael-guzman">Abimael Guzmán</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a>. It launched an armed struggle in 1980, and the conflict with the state cost some 70,000 lives by official estimates. The movement collapsed after Guzmán's capture in 1992, though small remnants survived in remote valleys.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shining-path.jpg" alt="Shining Path" width="320" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cesar David MP · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_PCP-Shining_Path.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Shining Path, officially the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, was founded in 1969 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abimael-guzman">Abimael Guzmán</a>, a former university philosophy professor whom his followers called Presidente Gonzalo, together with 11 others. Guzmán was heavily influenced by a trip to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and admired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>. Its common name comes from a maxim of Mariátegui: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a> will open the shining path to revolution.</p><p>From 1970 to 1977 it built a student organization and regional committees in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> and the central sierra. It first gained a foothold at San Cristóbal of Huamanga University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a>, where Guzmán taught, and between 1973 and 1975 controlled the student councils at the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huancayo">Huancayo</a> and La Cantuta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By 1980 it had about 500 members. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s military government allowed elections for the first time in twelve years, the Shining Path declined to take part and began a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war in the highlands of the Ayacucho Region. On 17 May 1980, on the eve of the presidential elections, it burned ballot boxes in the town of Chuschi, its first act of war. Through the 1980s it grew in territory and militants, particularly in the Andean highlands, gaining some peasant sympathy by filling a political gap left by the central government and offering what it called popular justice: public trials with swift sentences, including public executions.</p><p>The government's response was initially tepid. On 29 December 1981 it declared an emergency zone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huancavelica">Huancavelica</a> and Apurímac and let the military detain any suspect. The military abused this power, arresting many innocent people and subjecting some to torture and rape.</p><p>On 12 September 1992 the Special Intelligence Group captured Guzmán and several leaders in an apartment above a dance studio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>. The leadership vacuum was large, and the organization suffered defeats from rural self-defence organizations of campesinos, supposedly its social base. When Guzmán called for peace talks, it split between those for and those against them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party was designed on a concentric model, with Communist Party organs at the centre, the paramilitary wing around them and the political wing on the outside, so that the party controlled both. Its ideology moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> thought to Marxism–Leninism–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maoism">Maoism</a>–Gonzalo thought, and some authors see a cult of personality around Guzmán.</p><p>It has been widely condemned for brutality against peasants, as in the Lucanamarca massacre, and against trade unionists, rival Marxist groups and elected officials. The Asháninka minority suffered disproportionately, with about 6,000 killed, over 10% of their 1993 population.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shining Path” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sino-Soviet split, open by the early 1960s, divided the communist world, and Mao’s doctrine of rural revolution inspired movements from India’s Naxalites to Peru’s <b>Shining Path</b>, while China backed communist forces in Vietnam and the Khmer Rouge in Cambodia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the twentieth century Peru fought Colombia over Leticia in 1932–1933 and Ecuador in 1941, 1981 and 1995, and from 1980 to 2000 the armed forces waged a brutal counter-insurgency against the <b>Shining Path</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Communist Party of the Philippines was founded on Mao’s birthday in 1968, and Peru’s <b>Shining Path</b> under Abimael Guzmán waged a prolonged people’s war.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
